Run `course start vision --gui` and wait for the startup checks to pass.
Run `course wall-follow` to start moving the robot.
Run `course stop` to close the simulation.

Marker navigation
=================

Build the package with `course build` and source `install/setup.bash`
from the workspace root.

1. Run `course start vision`, followed by `course wall-follow`.
2. Once the robot returns to the start, check the simulation log for
   "Saved marker route". The exporter writes `marker_route.csv` and
   `marker_route.start-map.csv` at the workspace root. A valid run needs
   four detected marker types and the start-time map transform.
3. Run `course save-map demo` while the mapping session is still active.
   Keep the two CSV files with this run's `maps/demo.yaml` and `maps/demo.pgm`.
   The main CSV has the odometry start x, y and heading in radians on line
   one, then four marker x, y and type rows in map coordinates.
4. Run `course stop`, then `course start navigation --map maps/demo.yaml`.
   Place the robot at the same starting position and heading as in step 1.
5. From the workspace root, run:

   ros2 run wall_follower navigate_markers.py --ros-args \
       -p use_sim_time:=true -p map_file:=maps/demo.yaml \
       -p marker_file:=marker_route.csv

   The node loads the specified map through Nav2, initializes localization
   with the saved map-frame start and visits each marker in CSV order.
   To demonstrate a different order, shuffle CSV lines 2 through 5.
   For a hand-authored CSV, use `-p odom_matches_map:=true` only when the
   recorded start coordinates and heading already match the saved map frame.
